{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Suddenly, unawares.</b> Like (Daniel 12) ״אדמת עפר״ (lit. 'ground of dust,' an attribute used as a noun). And behold, he brings an offering for the intentional as for the inadvertent, and so they said, of blessed memory, in chapter 2 of Keritot: four bring an offering for the intentional as for the inadvertent, and they are — the nazirite, the oath of the deposit, the oath of testimony, and the guilt-offering of a designated maidservant, for she is lashed yet he brings an offering. Concerning the nazirite it says ״בפתע״, which is inadvertent; ״פתאם״ is intentional, for it is said (Proverbs 22): ״ופתאים עברו ונענשו״ — treat the intentional as inadvertent. And likewise with the oath of testimony, Scripture did not mention ״ונעלם״ in it, whereas in all the others it mentioned ״ונעלם״; and in the oath of testimony it did not mention ״ונעלם״, to obligate for the intentional as for the inadvertent. The oath of the deposit is learned from the oath of testimony by a gezerah shavah of ״תחטא תחטא.״ In the case of one who lies with a designated maidservant, it says of him ״ונסלח לו״ (Leviticus 19), to make the intentional as the inadvertent — that is, that he is forgiven by this offering in whatever manner it may be, whether inadvertent or intentional."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The prooftext cited as Daniel 12 reads אדמת עפר, which appears in Daniel 12:2 (not 12:13); the reference may be approximate to the chapter. The phrase ונסלח לו is cited as Leviticus 19 but actually appears in Leviticus 19:22."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:02.357848653Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}